- The owner reported being logged into Zuzz, that the screen "looks great", and that the on-screen keyboard "looked amazing".
- Corroborated from the journal that Zuzz was launched from the desktop dock entry rather than by hand: `app-gnome-zuzz-14159.scope - Application launched by gnome-shell` at 09:07:22, immediately followed by `zuzz-media.service` starting. This is the first real-world use of the Spec 015 desktop launch path and it worked. The service had been left stopped by the preceding test, so the start was the owner's.
- Accepted as a result: Spec 015 T007's display settings (1.5 text scaling and 64px dock icons are readable at projector distance) and T008's desktop launcher; and the bulk of Spec 009 T011 (projector presentation, streaming login, and on-screen-keyboard text entry on the Firefox-based launcher).
- The on-screen keyboard result is worth noting against the project's history: Spec 009 spent significant effort failing to get GNOME's keyboard to appear in Brave, root-caused it to Chromium's Wayland text-input behaviour, and switched to Firefox on that basis. This is the first owner confirmation that the keyboard works well in the finished managed launcher, which validates that decision.
- **Not yet confirmed and still open**: whether the DualSense stick actually moves the pointer. The owner has not stated whether the dock icon was clicked with the controller or with a mouse, so the Spec 015 T012 pointer criterion cannot be marked accepted on this evidence. Also open: video and audio playback through the projector and receiver, and whether the Zuzz login persists across a close and relaunch.

## 2026-09-05 — Spec 015: Dock Pinned Open; Per-Key Baseline Capture Fixed

- Checked what the owner would actually see before writing test instructions. Ubuntu Dock is enabled and active and hot corners are on, so the pointer alone can reach both the dock and the Activities overview; the preset sends no Super key, so this mattered. But the dock defaulted to auto-hide, which means hunting for a screen edge with a controller stick from across the room.
- Pinned the dock open and enlarged its icons through the tracked settings script: `dash-to-dock dock-fixed` true (was false), `autohide` false (was true), `dash-max-icon-size` 64 (was 48).
- **Fixed a real flaw in `configure-desktop-home.sh` found while doing this.** `capture_baseline` guarded on the whole file, so once a baseline existed no further keys were ever captured. Any setting added to the script after the first `--apply` would have been applied with no recorded prior value and would not have been restored by `--revert`. Capture is now per key: existing keys keep their original captured value, and newly added keys are captured on the next run. Verified the three original values were preserved untouched while the three new ones were captured correctly.

## 2026-09-05 — Spec 015: Desktop Pointer Fixed; input-remapper Injection Had Never Worked

The owner asked whether the mouse is supposed to be movable with the DualSense. Checking rather than assuming exposed two defects, one of them mine and one long-standing.

- **Circular dependency introduced by Spec 015 (my error).** Pointer mode was turned on only by `launch-zuzz.sh --run` and turned off again by `--restore`. Under the Kodi home this was coherent, because Kodi used native joystick events through `kodi-peripheral-joystick` and did not need remapping. Spec 015 deleted Kodi and with it the thing that made the home navigable, but did not move the pointer mode in to fill the gap. The result was unusable: the controller was needed to reach the launcher that enabled the controller, and closing the browser took the pointer away again. Spec 015's own acceptance criterion requires the pointer to navigate the desktop, launch the browser, and return, so T012 would have failed when the owner tried it.
- Fixed with `config/systemd/user/dualsense-desktop-input.service`, which turns the mode on at login using a new `on-when-present` subcommand that waits up to 60 seconds for the Bluetooth controller and exits 0 if it never appears, so a missing controller does not fail the login. The browser no longer touches the mode; `--restore` and `zuzz-media.service`'s `ExecStopPost` were removed entirely, further simplifying the launcher.
- **input-remapper injection had never actually worked.** `input-remapper-control` printed `Starting injection ... Done` while the daemon logged `ERROR: "config.json" does not exist` and `ERROR: Request to start an injectoin before a user told the service about their session using set_config_dir`. The daemon rejects every request until a client names the session config directory, and will not accept that without `config.json`. The CLI reports success regardless, so the mode looked healthy and injected nothing. No virtual input devices existed. This predates Spec 015 and was never caught because Spec 008's acceptance validated native joystick navigation in Kodi, a completely different input path, and the docs recorded only that status was "cached intent, not live injection".
- Fixed in `dualsense-media-mode.sh`: it now creates `config.json` on first use with an autoload entry so the preset reapplies on controller reconnect, and passes `--config-dir` on every daemon call. Confirmed live: `input-remapper mouse`, `input-remapper keyboard`, and `input-remapper DualSense Wireless Controller forwarded` device nodes now exist while injecting.
- Replaced the `status` command's cached-intent report with a real liveness check. It tests for the per-device `... forwarded` node, which exists only while this controller is being injected; the generic `input-remapper mouse` and `keyboard` nodes are not valid signals because they persist once created. Verified the command reports correctly in both the on and off states and exits non-zero when the mode is requested but not injecting.
- Added `scripts/install-desktop-home.sh` to install the pointer service and helper symlinks and apply the display settings; verified idempotent and confirmed it never enables `media-home.service`. Also fixed `dualsense-media-mode.sh` to resolve its own path through symlinks with `readlink -f`, which it needed once installed into `~/.local/bin`.
- Validated end to end: stopping and starting the login service turns the mode off and on with live injection confirmed each time, and a full browser start/stop cycle leaves the pointer untouched and `media-home.service` disabled and inactive throughout.
- T012 owner couch acceptance is now actually testable. It was not before.

