## 2026-09-04 — Spec 009 T008/T009 Root-Cause Isolation: GNOME Shell Bug, Not a Brave Flag Gap

- Relaunched the managed browser twice with owner acceptance testing: GNOME's on-screen keyboard correctly appears for the native GTK keyring-unlock dialog but never for Brave's own web-content text fields, even after adding `--enable-wayland-ime --wayland-text-input-version=3` (T008) and `--force-renderer-accessibility` (T009).
- Captured a `WAYLAND_DEBUG=1` protocol trace of a manual, unmanaged Brave instance (same flags) while the owner tapped a Zuzz text field. Brave correctly bound `zwp_text_input_manager_v3`, created a `zwp_text_input_v3` object, and sent the full expected handshake on focus: `enter()`, `enable()`, `set_content_type()`, `set_cursor_rectangle()`, `set_surrounding_text()`, `commit()`.
- Conclusion: Brave/Chromium's Wayland text-input-v3 client implementation is working correctly and telling the compositor a text field is focused. The keyboard's failure to appear is a GNOME Shell/Mutter-side defect in reacting to that signal, not a missing or incorrect Brave launch flag. Further Brave flag changes are not expected to fix this.
- Cross-checked a public report (Brave community forum, "onscreen keyboard not triggered on Ubuntu") describing the same symptom with no resolution posted before the thread auto-closed — consistent with an unresolved upstream issue rather than a local misconfiguration.
- Also confirmed as background facts during diagnosis: session is native GNOME Wayland (not X11), `ibus-x11` bridge is running but no non-default input engine is selected (`xkb us` only), and no `wayland-info`-equivalent tool was available locally for deeper protocol inspection beyond the captured trace.
- Operational note: the manual debug Brave instance's child process outlived an initial `pkill` pattern match (it targeted the `brave-browser` wrapper script, not the re-exec'd `/opt/brave.com/brave/brave` binary), causing the managed service's next start to silently hand off to the still-running instance and report itself inactive. Resolved by killing all processes matching the profile's `--user-data-dir`, clearing the stale `SingletonLock`, and cleanly restarting `brave-media.service`, which is confirmed active and normal.
- `--force-renderer-accessibility` was left in `scripts/launch-zuzz.sh`'s `--run` launch command; it is harmless and does not regress anything, but did not resolve the keyboard gap.
- Decision needed from the owner before continuing T008/T009: whether to attempt a standalone on-screen keyboard app (new dependency requiring approval) as a stopgap, or defer text entry without a physical keyboard to the already-planned Spec 010 controller-driven keyboard, closing T008/T009 as a documented upstream limitation.

## 2026-09-04 — Spec 009 Firefox On-Screen-Keyboard Comparison Test

- At the owner's suggestion, ran a manual, unmanaged `firefox --kiosk https://zuzz.tv` test (snap Firefox 154.0, already installed) in place of Brave, with `brave-media.service` stopped for the duration.
- The owner confirmed the GNOME on-screen keyboard correctly appears and is usable in a Firefox text field on the same site, unlike Brave/Chromium under identical GNOME accessibility settings.
- This narrows T008/T009's root cause: it is not a blanket GNOME Shell defect (Firefox proves the compositor-side trigger works), but a Brave/Chromium-specific incompatibility in how its Wayland text-input-v3 client behaves, despite the protocol trace looking superficially correct.
- The diagnostic Firefox process was stopped after the test; no persistent Firefox launcher, profile, or service was created. `brave-media.service` was left stopped pending the owner's decision on whether to keep pursuing Brave or move Spec 009's browser choice to Firefox.

## 2026-09-04 — Architectural Decision: Desktop-First Home, Kodi Retirement, and Phone-Based Input

- The project owner decided to retire the Kodi-first media home in favor of booting to the ordinary GNOME desktop, on the stated basis that the appliance will never hold a local media library. Kodi's remaining value was as a launcher shell, which the desktop provides directly.
- The owner also chose phone-based remote input (KDE Connect / GSConnect) over continuing to pursue on-screen-keyboard support, following the Spec 009 finding that GNOME's keyboard depends on application cooperation that Brave/Chromium does not provide.
- Created `specs/0/0.015-desktop-first-home/spec.md`. It amends Spec 006 (automatic login is retained; only the Kodi-launch half of `media-home.service` retires), supersedes Spec 007 and most of Spec 012, reduces Spec 010, and simplifies Spec 011. Kodi stays installed and launchable so the decision remains reversible.
- Created `specs/0/0.016-gsconnect-remote-input/spec.md` for phone-based keyboard and pointer input using Ubuntu's `gnome-shell-extension-gsconnect` (`71-1ubuntu1`, confirmed available; not installed). No third-party package source is required.
- Recorded rationale: the browser/Kodi stop-restore orchestration is the appliance's most failure-prone component and caused two visible faults during this session alone, in addition to the previously documented Kodi bounded-stop and HDMI-sink failures. The desktop-first change is subtractive, since Kodi already runs as a window inside the normal GNOME Wayland session rather than as a standalone session.
- Recorded risks: GNOME is not a ten-foot interface and will need display scaling and large-text settings for projector distance; and the KDE Connect iOS client has historically lacked remote input, so the owner's phone platform must be confirmed before Spec 016 implementation.
- The owner directed that Spec 015 be specified before Spec 016. No implementation of either feature has begun, and no roadmap status fields have been changed yet; those edits are tasks within Spec 015.
- Spec 009's Firefox switch remains uncommitted on `feature/0.009-brave-zuzz-browser-streaming` at the time of this entry.
